</style>

<style>
	/* ---- title card + index -------------------------------------- */

	.wall-head {
		display: flex;
		justify-content: flex-end;
		align-items: flex-start;
		flex-wrap: wrap;
		column-gap: calc(var(--baseline) * 2);
		row-gap: var(--baseline);
		padding-block-start: calc(var(--baseline) * 2);
	}

	.wall-head__card {
		width: fit-content;
		max-width: 100%;
	}

	/* the language tab: pinned beside the title card, dropped a step so
	   it reads as its own chit; wraps under the card when the wall is
	   too narrow for the pair — still inside the first viewport */
	.wall-head :global(.wall-head__tab) {
		margin-block-start: var(--baseline);
		/* Typed paper, so it is typed at the TYPED SETTING — the same
		   step the record card below it is struck at. The tab used to run
		   at 0.875rem / 0.04em: a third Courier size and a third Courier
		   tracking in one viewport, on a chit sitting inches from the
		   title, which made a language switcher look like its own
		   typeface. Worse, 0.875rem put it dead level with every
		   handwritten note on the wall.

		   It costs no room: Courier's advance is 0.6em, so the size step
		   adds 0.6px per character and the tracking removal gives back
		   0.56px — the tab is measured 0.5px wider. The control's own
		   signals are untouched (inert language soft and light, the
		   destination bold in thread red under the house link underline,
		   the inked exchange arrow between them); only the face's setting
		   joins the system. */
		font-size: var(--step-0);
		letter-spacing: normal;
	}

	/* THE NAME-PLATE, NOT THE HEADLINE. This sat at clamp(2rem, 4vw,
	   2.75rem) — 44px, the largest type in the first viewport, larger
	   than the signature and larger than the claim. It names the LAYOUT
	   METAPHOR, not the offer: a wall's title card is a name-plate, and a
	   name-plate that outranks everything pinned to the wall tells the
	   reader the filing system is the subject. Demoted to --step-2 it
	   still opens the sheet and still carries the index under it, and the
	   top of the ladder is freed for the man's own signature. */
	.wall-head__title {
		margin: 0;
		font-size: var(--step-2);
		line-height: var(--lh-2);
	}

	.wall-head__rule {
		display: block;
		width: 8.5rem;
		margin-block: calc(var(--baseline) * 0.75) var(--baseline);
	}

	.wall-head nav ul {
		list-style: none;
		margin: 0;
		padding: 0;
		display: flex;
		flex-wrap: wrap;
		column-gap: calc(var(--baseline) * 3);
	}

	/* The index, in the flyer's own press at the flyer's own tracking.
	   THIS RULE SETS NO LETTER-SPACING, and that absence is the fix. The
	   sheet declares its tracking once — `.pin-card--flyer` — and it
	   inherits down to every line on the sheet, the title included. This
	   row used to override it at 0.045em, which opened a gap 6.2× the
	   headline's own out of one League Gothic sheet. A reader has no name
	   for that, so they read it as a second face. Now nothing on the
	   sheet overrides the sheet. */
	.wall-head nav a {
		display: inline-block;
		padding-block: calc(var(--baseline) * 1.5);
		font-size: var(--step-1);
		line-height: var(--lh-1);
		color: var(--ink);
