	   Net +30px of usable first screen, minus the only labelled navigation
	   the phone has. It was not worth it, and the reorder in Hero.astro
	   cleared the acceptance target without it.
	   WHAT IS DONE IS THE AIR, and every value below is a baseline multiple
	   so the rhythm survives a reader's own type size: the sheet sits one
	   baseline off the viewport's top edge instead of two, the name-plate
	   takes --lh-1 (its League Gothic caps measure 21.1px at this width, so a
	   24px line box holds them with room — verified on the render at 390 and
	   at 1023, where the setting is tightest at 23.5px), the thread's squiggle
	   keeps half a baseline either side, the index's links take 1.25
	   baselines of padding, and the language chit hangs off the sheet on the
	   flex row-gap alone.
	   MEASURED AFTER: 277.3 → 239.3px (28.3 % of the viewport), the name at
	   y 308 (36.5 %), and the gap between the chit's bottom edge and the
	   board's top edge is unchanged at 23.0px — which is what the hero's
	   entrance thread hangs its 17px bend clearance on, so that stays intact.
	   THE INDEX'S TARGETS STAY OVER 44px. 1.25 baselines is 10px a side
	   against a 24.9px line box: 44.9px measured at root 16, and the box only
	   grows from there, since both terms scale with the reader's type. 44 is
	   SC 2.5.5's floor and this wall meets it everywhere else; it is not
	   spent here to buy 8px. */
	@media (max-width: 63.9375rem) {
		.wall-head {
			justify-content: center;
			padding-block-start: var(--baseline);
		}

		.wall-head__title {
			line-height: var(--lh-1);
		}

		.wall-head__rule {
			margin-block: calc(var(--baseline) * 0.5);
		}

		.wall-head nav a {
			padding-block: calc(var(--baseline) * 1.25);
		}

		.wall-head :global(.wall-head__tab) {
			margin-block-start: 0;
		}
	}

	/* ---- hero board → timeline spine ------------------------------ */
	/* Pulled up by the hero's bottom padding (baseline × 10) so the top
	   edge sits exactly on the hero board's bottom edge, where its
	   thread ends. Shares the hero board's shell (78rem, centered) so
	   viewBox fractions and board fractions coincide. */

	.stitch-join {
		width: 100%;
		max-width: 78rem;
		height: 224px;
		margin-top: calc(var(--baseline) * -10);
		margin-inline: auto;
		/* the same inline padding `.wall-max` carries, so the box below
		   reproduces the wall's content box exactly */
		padding-inline: calc(var(--baseline) * 3);
		pointer-events: none;
		/* behind the paper: the taut connector overlaps the hero's bottom
		   edge, and the thread never crosses printed ink */
		z-index: -1;
		position: relative;
	}

	/* the header run replaces this connector when it routes through the
	   first district sign (see the builder). It is emptied of ink rather
	   than removed: the runtime run reads its start point off this path,
	   so the geometry has to stay laid out. */
	.stitch-join[data-routed] :global(.thread-path) {
		stroke: none;
	}

	/* THE COORDINATE FRAME, AND IT IS LOAD-BEARING. An absolutely
	   positioned child resolves its offsets against its containing
	   block's PADDING box, so padding on `.stitch-join` alone would not
	   move anything: the frame has to be an element. This one's padding
	   box IS `.wall-max`'s content box — the same box `.rf-line` and the
	   timeline's card stitches are positioned in — which is the whole
	   reason a single `--spine-x` can be read in both files and mean the
	   same pixel. */
	.stitch-join__box {
		position: relative;
		height: 100%;
	}

	.stitch-join__box > div {
		position: absolute;
		top: 0;
		height: 100%;
	}

	.stitch-join :global(svg) {
		width: 100%;
		height: 100%;
	}

	/* ≥64rem: the wall's board is wide, the hero exits at 802/1200 of it
	   and the spine stands at `--spine-x`. The hero's thread spans the
	   board's PADDING box (`inset: 0` on `.hero__board`), so its exit
	   fraction has to be re-expressed in this frame's content box:
	   exit = 0.6683·(W + 48) − 24 = 0.6683·W + 8.08, hence a right offset
	   of (1 − 0.6683)·W − 8.08. The 48 and the 24 are `.wall-max`'s own
	   padding; the 802/1200 is Hero.astro's last station. Both ends of
	   this box are therefore quoted from the contracts they meet, and
	   neither is a number chosen here. */
	.stitch-join__wide {
		display: none;
		left: var(--spine-x);
		right: calc(33.16667% - 8.08px);
	}

	/* <64rem: the hero stacks and exits at the shell's centre, the spine
	   stands to its left — one diagonal, at both narrow widths. */
	.stitch-join__stack {
		left: var(--spine-x);
		right: 50%;
	}

	@media (min-width: 64rem) {
		.stitch-join__stack {
			display: none;
		}

		.stitch-join__wide {
			display: block;
		}
	}

	/* stretched boxes: disable the dash (Chromium dash-normalization bug
	   under non-scaling-stroke) and wipe-reveal instead */
	.stitch-join__wide :global(.thread-path),
	.stitch-join__stack :global(.thread-path) {
		stroke-dasharray: none;
	}

	/* PACING — the hero is the establishing shot, not a scroll beat.
	   Scroll-linking this thread was wrong in both directions. Linked to
	   cover 0 % it was 88 % drawn before the visitor moved, so it led
	   nowhere; pushed out to cover 35 % it left the FIRST VIEWPORT — the
	   one image that decides what this site is — with barely a quarter of
	   its own concept visible, and every static capture of the fold shows
	   that quarter. The hero's six pins are all on screen at once, so
	   there is nothing here to lead anyone toward: the leading is done by
	   the 25 legs below, which are scroll-drawn and arrive just ahead of
	   the reader.
	   So the hero draws itself ONCE, on arrival, in its own time — a
	   signature being written rather than a diagram being revealed — and
	   is complete and legible a second later, in the browser and in any
	   screenshot alike. */
	/* Gated exactly like every other motion on this wall: the fully-drawn
	   thread stays the CSS default, and this only rewinds it for a visitor
	   who welcomes motion. */
	@media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
		:global(.hero__thread .thread-path--draw) {
			animation: thread-draw 1100ms cubic-bezier(0.215, 0.61, 0.355, 1) 120ms both;
			animation-timeline: initial;
			animation-range: normal;
		}
	}

	@supports (animation-timeline: view()) {
		@media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
			/* the same reveal law as the lower run and the timeline's own
			   entry line — `entry 0% entry 85%`, so this seam is also drawn
			   BELOW the fold rather than finishing at 55 % of the screen
			   like the old `cover 38% cover 56%` did. Three mechanisms
			   (clip wipe, scale, dash) now share one calibration. */
			.stitch-join__wide,
			.stitch-join__stack {
				clip-path: inset(0 0 100% 0);
				animation: stitch-wipe linear both;
				animation-timeline: view();
				animation-range: entry 0% entry 85%;
			}
		}
	}

	@keyframes stitch-wipe {
		to {
			clip-path: inset(-8px);
		}
	}

	/* ---- the long run --------------------------------------------- */
	/* No line of its own any more: the run is the `.wall-stitch` layer,
	   measured from the pins. `.wall-run` stays as the acts' container
	   and keeps its own stacking context so nothing inside it can slip
	   below the thread. */

	.wall-run {
		position: relative;
		isolation: isolate;
	}

	/* ---- act breaks ------------------------------------------------ */
	/* Hero → Timeline always had a designed scene change (`.stitch-join`,
	   224px). Timeline → Projects, Projects → Telemetry and Telemetry →
	   Practice had none: seven unbroken screens at 46–54 % ink coverage
	   with no rest anywhere in them. This is that scene change, three
	   more times: a band of bare cork with the thread visibly crossing it
	   and nothing else on it. It is the only place on this wall allowed
	   to be empty, which is what makes the emptiness read as design. */

	.act-break {
		height: calc(var(--baseline) * 16);
		pointer-events: none;
	}

	/* 48.0625rem — see the `--spine-x` block above: the 48rem pair was not
	   disjoint, and this was one of the two rules that made 768 taller than
	   both of its neighbours. */
	@media (min-width: 48.0625rem) {
		.act-break {
			height: calc(var(--baseline) * 25);
		}
	}

	/* ---- the progress rail ----------------------------------------- */
	/* Navigation used to exist for 1.6 % of the page: the index at
	   y 88–177 and then nothing but a 44px back-to-top pin for 11,000px.
	   The rail is the same pin, in the same corner, hung on the thread:
	   its offset IS scroll progress, and the section offsets are ticks you
	   can click. The page's only permanent UI is now made of the page's own
	   idea.
	   THREE RANKS OF ONE MARK, AND EVERY RULE BELOW SERVES THEM: unlit ink
	   is terrain the reader has not walked, `--thread` is terrain they have,
	   brass is where they are standing. It is the wall's own law — up there
	   the thread's EXTENT is the story so far — restated at 1:68. */

	.rail {
		--rail-line: 38px;
		--rail-h: 180px;
		/* the chit's slot at the FOOT of the scale. Same name, same job as
		   the narrow strip's: the length of the scale that belongs to the
		   address rather than to the districts, so no tick and no index can
		   ever stand on it. On this axis it is 40px of height; on the
		   narrow strip it is 152px of width. */
		--mail-slot: calc(var(--baseline) * 5);
		/* the last district's offset clamps to 1.0, so its graduation stands
		   exactly on the track's end. Without this the chit's top edge cut
		   that mark in half — the one district sign the strip could not
		   show is the conversion event's own. */
		--mail-gap: calc(var(--baseline) * 1.5);
		position: fixed;
		right: calc(var(--baseline) * 2);
		bottom: calc(var(--baseline) * 3);
		z-index: 50;
		width: 52px;
		/* the scale keeps its 180px; the chit and its gap are added below,
		   so the reduction the ticks and the pin share is unchanged */
		height: calc(var(--rail-h) + var(--mail-gap) + var(--mail-slot));
		pointer-events: none;
		visibility: hidden;
		opacity: 0;
		transition: visibility 0s var(--t-settle);
	}

	.rail[data-visible="true"] {
		visibility: visible;
		opacity: 1;
		transition: none;
	}

	@media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
		.rail {
			transition:
				opacity var(--t-settle) var(--e-out),
				visibility 0s var(--t-settle);
		}

		.rail[data-visible="true"] {
			transition: opacity var(--t-settle) var(--e-out);
		}
	}

	/* NOT `--thread-w`, and deliberately. The wall's thread went to 4px
	   because 2.5 was a hairline at 1:1; this line is the same thread at
	   68:1 — 12,173px of page compressed into 180px — and at that
	   reduction the wall's own weight reads as a bar rather than as a
	   thread. The rail is a scale drawing, so it keeps the scale's stroke.
	   Its ticks are sized against this line, not against the token. */
	/* AND CRIMSON MEANS HERE WHAT IT MEANS ON THE WALL. This was one bar of
	   `--thread` from end to end, and it measured identically at
	   `--progress: 0` and at 1. Forty pixels away, `path.thread-path` is the
	   SAME token at 4px — and there the EXTENT IS THE MEANING: the wall's
	   thread is drawn progressively and stops where the story has got to. One
	   graphic form carrying opposite semantics in a single frame is not a
	   style question. It teaches the reader a rule on the wall and breaks it
	   in the margin, forty pixels later.
	   So the scale now obeys the wall's own law at 1:68. An UNLIT GROOVE for
	   the route not yet walked — the wall's shadow ink, which is what a
	   channel cut in a board looks like — and `--thread` laid into it for the
	   part that has been. Measured: the groove composites to 1.70:1 against
	   the shelf's paper (1.69:1 at the lamp's dark end) and 1.34:1 on bare
	   cork. That is a rest state and it is not claimed as an indicator; the
	   ticks standing on it are the indicators and they carry their own
	   contrast — see `.rail__tick::after`. */
	.rail__line {
		position: absolute;
		top: 0;
		/* THE RULE ENDS WHERE THE JOURNEY ENDS. It ran `bottom: 0` — the full
		   232px, the address's slot included — while the brass index travels
		   only the 180px tick track. Measured: the marker stops at 71.6 % of
		   the rule the eye reads as its track, so a reader who trusts the rule
		   believes 28 % of the wall is still ahead of them when none of it is.
		   Under the exit gradient that is the expensive direction of error:
		   readers abandon when they think more remains than does. */
		bottom: calc(var(--mail-slot) + var(--mail-gap));
		left: calc(var(--rail-line) - 0.75px);
		width: 1.5px;
		border-radius: 2px;
		background: oklch(var(--shade) / 0.25);
	}

	/* the walked part: the wall's own thread, at the wall's own reduction,
	   laid into the groove.
	   ITS TIP IS UNDER THE BRASS AT EVERY PROGRESS, ON BOTH AXES, AND THAT IS
	   ARITHMETIC RATHER THAN LUCK. The head travels `progress · (track − 28)`
	   from the track's start, so tip − head's far edge = 28·(progress − 1) ≤ 0
	   and tip − head's near edge = 28·progress ≥ 0. The drawn end of the
	   thread is inside the 28px disc for every value of progress, which is
	   why the two mechanisms that report position can never be SEEN to
	   disagree — the same guarantee the wall gives by running its thread
	   under every pin head it passes. */
	.rail__line::after {
		content: "";
		position: absolute;
		top: 0;
		left: -0.5px;
		width: 2.5px;
		height: calc(var(--progress, 0) * 100%);
		border-radius: 2px;
		background: var(--thread);
	}

	/* the ticks' track: on this axis, the scale minus the address's own end
	   of it. The ticks and the pin both measure themselves against it, so
	   they can never disagree about where the reader is — and `.rail__line`
	   now stops here too, so neither can they disagree with the rule they
	   are drawn on. (The narrow strip resolves the same conflict the other
	   way: it moves the address off the axis and gives the track the whole
	   width. See that block.) */
	.rail__ticks {
		position: absolute;
		inset: 0 0 calc(var(--mail-slot) + var(--mail-gap, 0px)) 0;
		list-style: none;
		margin: 0;
		padding: 0;
	}

	/* 24×24 target, a 14px dash standing CLEAR OF THE DISC.
	   The tick used to sit at `--rail-line - 24px`, i.e. its right edge on
	   the thread — which is also where the 28px brass pin rides. The pin
	   occludes anything within 14px of its centre, so the tick that says
	   where you are was the one tick you could not see, and from progress
	   ≈0.93 to the end BOTH the last two ticks (22px apart) were under it.
	   Moved to the rail's left edge the whole 24px target is disjoint from
	   the disc's 28px column: the dash now reads as a graduation BESIDE the
	   scale rather than a mark crossing it, which is what a scale drawing
	   with a travelling index actually looks like. Reaching the line and
	   staying clear of a disc centred on the line is not a thing that has a
	   solution. */
	/* 24 × 20, NOT 24 × 24: `How I work` and `Contact` stand about 23px
	   apart on this 180px scale, so square targets overlapped by 0.7px on
	   the English wall at 1200, 1440 and 2560 and by 0.6 at 1024 — with
	   `Contact` painting over `How I work`, because it comes later. The
	   German wall cleared by 0.2–0.5px at the same widths, which is exactly
	   why this stays invisible until it is measured in BOTH locales. The
	   OFFSETS may not move: they are the scale, and they are what makes the
	   ticks and the index agree. So the boxes lose 4px of height. 22 would
	   have been enough for today's page (0.0px clear at 1440) and that is
	   not a margin — the offsets move whenever the wall's content does. At
	   20 the worst case measures 2.0px clear.
	   SC 2.5.8's Equivalent exception carries the shortfall against 24 × 24:
	   the title card's directory holds five compliant controls to the same
	   five destinations, and the sixth — the origin mark — has the skip
	   link, which is a full-size control to the same `#main`. */
	.rail__tick {
		position: absolute;
		top: calc(var(--at, 0) * 100%);
		left: 0;
		width: 24px;
		height: 20px;
		margin-top: -10px;
		pointer-events: auto;
		display: grid;
		place-items: center end;
	}

	/* THE GRADUATIONS LEAVE THE THREAD'S COLOUR TO THE THREAD. They were
	   `--thread` at 55 %, which put five crimson marks BEYOND the drawn tip
	   above — the same "one form, two meanings" defect restated at 14px, and
	   the reason it survived the first reading is that it is small. Unlit
	   ink for a district that exists, crimson for the one you are standing
	   in, brass for exactly where you stand: one law, three ranks.
	   IT IS ALSO THE CONTRAST FIX, AND THAT WAS NOT THE INTENTION. A tick is
	   a state indicator, so SC 1.4.11 wants 3:1 against its ground. Measured
	   composites: the old crimson-at-55 % read 2.79:1 on the shelf's paper
	   and 2.06:1 on the desktop rail's bare cork — a fail on both grounds,
	   which no one had computed because the mark was made of a token that
	   passes at full strength. Shadow ink at 55 % reads 3.82:1 on paper,
	   3.74:1 at the lamp's dark end, and 3.06:1 on cork. The current mark is
	   `--thread` at full strength: 6.18:1 on paper, 3.13:1 on cork. */
	.rail__tick::after {
		content: "";
		display: block;
		width: 14px;
		height: 2.5px;
		border-radius: 2px;
		background: oklch(var(--shade) / 0.55);
	}

	/* scale, not width: the current tick changes on every scroll frame
	   that crosses a section boundary, and a width change re-lays out */
	.rail__tick:hover::after,
	.rail__tick:focus-visible::after,
	.rail__tick[aria-current="true"]::after {
		scale: 1.4 1;
		background: var(--thread);
	}

	/* THE NAMES WERE EXPOSED TO EVERYONE EXCEPT THE PEOPLE LOOKING AT THEM.
	   `.rail__label` was clipped to 1 × 1px permanently, with no hover
	   reveal and no `title`, so assistive technology got five named
	   destinations and a sighted mouse user got five identical dashes —
	   the accessible name was better served than sight, which is a rare
	   way round and not a good one. The name now slips out on hover and on
	   keyboard focus as `paper-slip`, the wall's own material, opening
	   LEFTWARDS into the cork margin so it never leaves the viewport.
	   It is still `clip-path`-hidden at rest, so nothing is announced
	   twice and the rail keeps its 52px footprint. */
	.rail__label {
		position: absolute;
		right: calc(100% + var(--baseline));
		top: 50%;
		translate: 0 -50%;
		width: 1px;
		height: 1px;
		/* the cap lives on the element, not on the reveal, so the narrow
		   rail's container-relative cap can beat it on equal specificity.
		   A district name is two or three words; this only stops a long
		   German one sprawling across the board. The longest string on
		   either wall is `WIE ICH ARBEITE` — 15 characters of League Gothic
		   at `--step--1`'s upper clamp (14px) plus `--track-display`, about
		   78px — so the cap should never fire. `text-overflow` is here for
		   the day it does: a name cut mid-word with no sign that anything
		   was cut is the failure mode this file exists to refuse. */
		max-width: 12rem;
		text-overflow: ellipsis;
		overflow: hidden;
		clip-path: inset(50%);
		white-space: nowrap;
		font-family: var(--font-display);
		font-size: var(--step--1);
		line-height: 1;
		letter-spacing: var(--track-display);
		text-transform: uppercase;
		color: var(--ink);
	}

	/* AND THE CURRENT ONE IS NOT A REVEAL AT ALL — IT IS THE SIGN. Measured at
	   1440×900 at scroll y 3,000 / 9,000 / 13,500:
	   `.rail__tick[aria-current] .rail__label` was 1×1px under
	   `clip-path: inset(50%)` at every one of them. No desktop frame showed a
	   district name anywhere in the rail — five 24×20 dashes, a brass marker
	   and the `Write to me` chit, and nothing else. The phone build has
	   painted the current district in a 61×21 paper chit since the strip
	   existed, so the two widths differed by a whole class of sign: desktop
	   offered orientation (a scale) and direction (a marker travelling on it)
	   and no IDENTIFICATION at all. A reader 9,000px in had a marker on a
	   scale and no name for where they were standing.
	   The chit opens leftwards into the cork, where the wall's own margin is,
	   tucked against the marker at the tick's own height — so it names the
	   graduation it belongs to rather than reporting from a corner. */
	.rail__tick:hover .rail__label,
	.rail__tick:focus-visible .rail__label {
		width: auto;
		height: auto;
		clip-path: none;
		padding: calc(var(--baseline) * 0.5) var(--baseline);
		background-color: var(--paper);
		background-image: var(--lamp);
		box-shadow: var(--pin-shadow);
	}

	/* THE PERMANENT REVEAL NEEDS CORK TO OPEN INTO, AND BELOW 86rem THERE IS
	   NONE. Naming the current district is the fix for a whole missing sign
	   class on desktop — the rail carried orientation and direction and no
	   IDENTIFICATION, so a reader 9,000px in had a marker on a scale and no
	   name for where they stood. But "there is 1,372px of clear board" is the
	   rail's left edge, not evidence the board is clear: below 1,376px the
	   shell is viewport-limited and the cork margin closes.
	   Measured overlap of the revealed chit against body text: 1,173px2 at
	   1024, 187px2 at 1200, and 0 at 1440. So the permanent chit is gated to
	   86rem, where the margin actually exists.
	   Hover and focus keep revealing it at every width, deliberately: those
	   are transient and reader-initiated, so a moment's overlap is a cost the
	   reader chose. A permanently parked chit sitting on someone's prose is
	   not. Below 86rem the narrow strip's own label rule takes over, which
	   has a home of its own in the top row and does not overlap anything. */
	@media (min-width: 86rem) {
		.rail__tick[aria-current="true"] .rail__label {
			width: auto;
			height: auto;
			clip-path: none;
			padding: calc(var(--baseline) * 0.5) var(--baseline);
			background-color: var(--paper);
			background-image: var(--lamp);
			box-shadow: var(--pin-shadow);
		}
	}

	/* THE CHIT AT THE FOOT OF THE THREAD. On desktop the rail's scale is a
	   180px column and seventeen Courier characters need 138.6px of it
	   lying the wrong way, so this end of the wall's thread carries the
	   INVITATION in the display face and the address itself waits at the
	   destination. It is the same object as the phone's chip — paper, the
	   lamp, the wall's cast shadow — pinned at the point the thread stops,
	   which is the whole thesis of the wall stated at 1:68 scale. */
	/* IT IS EXACTLY AS WIDE AS THE RAIL, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE CONSTRAINT.
	   Scenography sized this against "the 96px cork margin at 1440" — a
	   figure that only exists at 1440 and above: the shell is `max-width:
	   78rem`, so below 1376px it is viewport-limited and there is no cork
	   margin to clear at all. Measured on a chit set to its own content
	   width, over 16 scroll offsets at four widths in both locales: 0 of 16
	   frames collide at 1440 and 2560, but **5 of 16 collide at 1024 and
	   1200, up to 99 % of the chit covered, over body copy in 4–6 frames
	   and 2,215px² at worst.** So the chit is not allowed its own width. It
	   takes the rail's 52px column and sets two lines instead — a footprint
	   the wall already accepts at every width, because the thread, the
	   ticks and the brass index have stood in it since the rail existed. */
	.rail__mail {
		position: absolute;
		left: 0;
		right: 0;
		bottom: 0;
		height: var(--mail-slot);
		display: grid;
		place-items: center;
		padding-inline: calc(var(--baseline) * 0.5);
		pointer-events: auto;
		text-align: center;
		text-wrap: balance;
		background-color: var(--paper);
		background-image: var(--lamp);
		box-shadow: var(--pin-shadow);
		font-family: var(--font-display);
		font-size: var(--step--1);
		line-height: 1;
		letter-spacing: var(--track-display);
		text-transform: uppercase;
		color: var(--ink);
		text-decoration: none;
	}

	/* the label the width chooses; the other is not merely invisible but
	   absent, so the accessible name is never the two of them read out
	   one after the other */
	.rail__mail-addr {
		display: none;
	}

	.rail__mail:hover .rail__mail-say {
		text-decoration: underline;
		text-decoration-color: var(--thread);
		text-decoration-thickness: 2px;
		text-underline-offset: 4px;
	}

	/* the brass pin, riding the thread at scroll progress. Moved with
	   `translate`, not `top`: a fixed element that re-lays out on every
	   scroll frame files a layout-shift entry each time, and this page
	   is holding a 0.03 CLS. */
	.rail__pin {
		position: absolute;
		top: 0;
		translate: 0 calc(var(--progress, 0) * (var(--rail-h) - 28px));
		left: calc(var(--rail-line) - 14px);
		width: 28px;
		height: 28px;
		padding: 0;
		border: none;
		border-radius: 50%;
		cursor: pointer;
		pointer-events: auto;
		display: grid;
		place-items: center;
		color: var(--ink);
		background: radial-gradient(
			circle at 35% 30%,
			color-mix(in oklab, var(--brass) 40%, white),
			var(--brass) 58%,
			color-mix(in oklab, var(--brass) 55%, black)
		);
		box-shadow:
			2px 2px 4px oklch(var(--shade) / 0.34),
			inset 0 0 0 1px oklch(var(--shade) / 0.2);
	}

	.rail__pin svg {
		width: 15px;
		height: 15px;
	}

	/* A BRASS PIN IS 28px BECAUSE A BRASS PIN IS 28px. The disc's size is
	   the wall's material, not a target size, so the target is bought
	   separately: a transparent 44 × 44 pad centred on the head, which is
	   the size the narrow build's old corner button had and the size a
	   thumb wants. Nothing about the drawn object changes. */
	.rail__pin::before {
		content: "";
		position: absolute;
		top: 50%;
		left: 50%;
		width: 44px;
		height: 44px;
		translate: -50% -50%;
	}

	/* the press-in law: nothing lifts, nothing glows — the cast shrinks */
	.rail__pin:hover {
		box-shadow:
			1px 1px 3px oklch(var(--shade) / 0.34),
			inset 0 0 0 1px oklch(var(--shade) / 0.2);
	}

	.rail__pin:active {
		box-shadow:
			0 0 2px oklch(var(--shade) / 0.34),
			inset 0 0 0 1px oklch(var(--shade) / 0.2);
	}

	/* ---- the rail on a narrow wall: same signage, turned 90° --------- */
	/* IT WAS SWITCHED OFF HERE, WHICH IS WHERE IT WAS NEEDED MOST. This
	   query used to read `.rail__line, .rail__ticks { display: none }`, so
	   at 390 all five ticks computed 0 × 0 and the wall's only permanent
	   control was a back-to-top pin: on a page 24 screens long whose only
	   conversion event is at the bottom, the one thing always on screen
	   pointed the wrong way, and the longest stretch with no landmark of
	   any kind measured 6,528px — 7.7 screens.
	   The reason given was that a narrow wall has no margin to hang a rail
	   in, and that reason is correct: at 390 the cards span 23–366 of a
	   390px viewport, so the right-hand cork is 24px wide and the fixed pin
	   was measured sitting on paper at 79 % of sampled scroll depths (0 %
	   at 1440). What was wrong was the conclusion. A wall with no vertical
	   margin still has a HORIZONTAL one, and the strip below is the same
	   four objects — thread, riding brass pin, five district ticks, and now
	   the address — laid along the axis that has 366px instead of the axis
	   that has 24.
	   IT IS STILL THE RAIL, not a borrowed progress bar: the line is the
	   wall's thread at the wall's reduction, the index is the brass pin
	   riding it at true scroll progress, and the ticks stand at the
	   districts' true proportional offsets — so pin and ticks agree about
	   where the reader is. Even spacing would have bought 44px targets and
	   made the two devices contradict each other on the one surface where
	   the scale drawing is the whole point.
	   And it is a pinned artefact: paper stock, the lamp, the wall's own
	   cast shadow, lying along the bottom edge of the board. */
	/* THE STRIP WAS A BAR AND IT SAT ON THE SCENT CHANNEL. As a full-width
	   60px sheet it painted 6.70 % of the viewport, permanently: over 60
	   evenly spaced offsets at 390 it covered body text at 53 and covered a
	   text node by 85 % or more at 17 — "Raleo Digital GmbH", "Columbus
	   Interactive GmbH", "Full-stack developer · since 2025". Forward scent
	   is read off the leading edge of a downward scroll, which is the
	   bottom 60–100px of the viewport, so the conversion affordance had
	   been parked on the channel that decides whether the reader gets far
	   enough to use it.
	   The bar is now two objects instead of one sheet:
	   — a PLINTH, 26px, full width: the only thing the scale needs, because
	     `--thread` on bare cork measures 1.60–2.84:1 and a graduation with
	     no ground is not a control anyone can see. It sits at the very
	     bottom of the strip's box, so it is under rather than across the
	     scent band;
	   — the CHIT, 44px, right-anchored: the conversion event, still the
	     largest target in the rail.
	   Everything between them is transparent and `pointer-events: none`,
	   so the wall shows through where the strip used to hide it — and a tap
	   in that band now reaches the wall instead of the strip.
	   After, same probe, same 60 offsets: 4.07 % of the viewport, mean area
	   covered 8,220 → 5,048px², nodes ≥85 % covered 17 → 7 (German 11 → 4),
	   and none of them a company name or a role.
	   IT IS STILL FULL WIDTH, and that is not a compromise but the
	   consequence: the ticks stay (they are the district index), so they
	   are state indicators and 1.4.11 applies to them, so they need a
	   ground — and their offsets are proportional to the page, so the
	   ground has to run the whole scale. The saving therefore has to come
	   out of the strip's HEIGHT, and it does. */
	/* ---- AND THEN IT BECAME TWO ROWS, FOR TWO MEASURED REASONS ---------
	   ONE. THE JOURNEY ENDED AT 55 % OF THE RULE THE EYE READS. Careful
	   here, because the obvious diagnosis is the wrong one: the tick offsets
	   ARE a correct linear normalisation of scroll — the measured `--at`
	   values match `sectionTop / (H − viewport)` to five decimals. The map
	   matches the terrain. What failed was the rule the map is drawn on. A
	   pixel scan of the rendered strip at 390 found crimson in three runs:
	   x 12→111.2 and x 139.8→233.8 (`.rail__line`, interrupted by the brass
	   marker) AND x 238.0→373.8 — the address chit's own `text-decoration`,
	   the same `oklch(0.5 0.19 22)` at 2px, co-linear with the rail rule to
	   within 0.25px and separated from it by a 4.2px gap. At arm's length
	   that is ONE continuous 361.8px rule on which the marker's travel ends
	   at 55.3 %, with the last district's graduation at x 207 and 167px of
	   visibly identical rule beyond it. Ticks and marker mapped onto
	   `x = 24 + at × 190`, because the track stopped at `--mail-slot`.
	   TWO. FORTY-TWO PER CENT OF EVERY TICK TARGET WAS NOT ON THE SCREEN.
	   The tick box was `top: 46px; height: 24px` inside a 60px strip seated
	   at `bottom: 0`, so it ran to strip-y 70 — ten pixels BELOW the
	   viewport's bottom edge. The comment that licensed it said the box runs
	   "into the margin the strip already reserves", and that confuses two
	   different margins: `body { padding-bottom }` reserves space in the
	   DOCUMENT, at its foot, once. It buys nothing at the foot of the
	   VIEWPORT, where a fixed strip lives. The real target was 24 × 14 —
	   below the AA floor the rule was written to meet, at every scroll
	   position, on every phone.
	   Both are the same fix, because both come from four objects competing
	   for one 60px band. The strip is now two rows:
	   — the TOP ROW, 34px: the district's own name printed at the left, the
	     address chit pinned at the right. Two signs, one row, and the chit's
	     underline is now 22px off the scale's axis instead of 0.25px.
	   — the BOTTOM ROW: the scale, and it spans the WHOLE painted width.
	     One span now serves the rule, the ticks and the marker, so the
	     journey ends where the rule ends and nothing crimson lies beyond it.
	   What it cost: 60 → 72px of strip, and the reserved gutter 72 → 84 to
	   match. What it bought, at 390: tick spacing 26.7 → 45.8px at the
	   tightest pair, so the targets go 24 × 14 (of which 24 × 14 on screen)
	   to 40 × 24, entirely on screen; and the chit 44 → 34px tall, which is
	   the one regression in the trade and is stated where it happens. */
	@media (max-width: 63.9375rem) {
		.rail {
			/* THE ADDRESS'S SHARE OF THE TOP ROW. It no longer bounds the
			   ticks' track — the track is the whole strip now, and that is
			   the point of the two rows — so what this protects is the room
			   left beside the address for the district's own name. The cap
			   still earns its keep and still earns it in the strip's own
			   unit: `9rem` alone is 152px at root 16 and 224px at root 24, so
			   on a 320px viewport an enlarged address would take the whole
			   shelf and leave the name nowhere to print. Capped at 45cqi it
			   can never take more than nine-twentieths of the strip. Same
			   rule as ever: the constraint is "share of this strip", so the
			   unit is the strip's. */
			--mail-slot: min(calc(9rem + var(--baseline)), 45cqi);
			/* ONE DECLARATION FOR THE TARGET BOX. Read by the tick's `width`,
			   by the mapping's own `margin-left`, and by the even-spacing
			   guard below — three places that must agree, and the previous
			   pair drifted the moment either was edited.
			   WHY 40 AND NOT 44: the tightest district pair is Δat = 0.1248
			   (How I work → Contact), so centre spacing over the full width
			   is (W − w) × 0.1248 — at W = 390, w = 44 that is 43.2px and two
			   44px targets OVERLAP by 0.8px, with the later one painting over
			   the earlier. At w = 40 it is 43.7px, 3.7px clear. 40 × 24 is
			   the honest maximum this scale admits without moving the
			   offsets, and the offsets are the drawing. */
			/* 32, NOT 40, AND THE SIXTH TICK IS WHY. 40 was derived from the
			   five-tick `--at` values quoted in the brief; measured on the
			   built page the origin tick added for the "wrong district for
			   2,555px" defect sits at 0.0119 and Career at 0.1169, so the
			   tightest pair is now Dat 0.105 in English and 0.1017 in German.
			   Centre spacing is Dat x (390 - w), so no-overlap needs
			   0.1017 x (390 - w) >= w, i.e. w <= 36.0 — and at 40 the targets
			   MEASURED -3.2px overlap in English and -4.4px in German, two
			   adjacent hit areas fighting for the same pixels at the one place
			   a reader most needs the first district.
			   32 leaves 4.4px clear on the German wall, which is the binding
			   locale. It is still 32 x 24, comfortably past SC 2.5.8's 24 x 24
			   floor, and the MARK is untouched at 2.5 x 12 — what shrank is
			   only the invisible area a thumb can hit, not anything drawn.
			   Re-measure this against BOTH locales if a tick is ever added or
			   the districts move: German runs tighter than English here, and
			   an English-only check would have shipped the overlap. */
			--tick-w: 32px;
			container-type: inline-size;
			/* FULL BLEED, ON THE EDGE. The strip used to float 12px clear of
			   the viewport on all three sides, which is what turned even a
			   solid shelf into a bisector: a line of text showed through the
			   12px gutters left and right AND through the 12px band below it,
			   so the reader saw the top of a sentence, then paper, then the
			   bottom of the same sentence. Measured at 200px steps over the
			   whole page, that was still 55 of 102 offsets after the ground
			   was made solid. Seated on the edge, a line is either above the
			   shelf and whole, or under it and gone. */
			left: 0;
			right: 0;
			bottom: 0;
			width: auto;
			/* 72, and `body { padding-bottom: 84px }` pays for it exactly.
			   The height is load-bearing in BOTH directions and the budget
			   is arithmetic, not taste. The tick lane must start below the
			   marker's centre and end above the viewport's edge, so
			   height ≥ topRow + 14 (the disc's radius) + 0.75 + 24 (the
			   target) — i.e. at 72 the top row may be 33.25. It is 34, and
			   the 0.75 it borrows is the brass head's outermost pixels
			   crossing the chit's bottom edge, which only happens at all past
			   progress 0.6 and looks like a pin lying on paper. Do not
			   re-derive this as 73: the tick box ends at 71.5, half a pixel
			   inside its own shelf, and what was squeezed is the row, not the
			   total. Every pixel above 72 is occlusion the reader pays for on
			   every screen, so there are none. */
			height: 72px;
			pointer-events: none;
			background: none;
			box-shadow: none;
		}

		/* THE PLINTH, AND WHY IT HAS AN EDGE. The wall's own 4px thread runs
		   down the board and is cut off wherever this strip stands. It used
		   to be cut off exactly where `.rail__line` — 2.5px of the SAME
		   `--thread` — ran horizontally through that point: same colour,
		   right angle, opaque paper hiding the crossing, so a wall whose
		   premise is one continuous line showed the reader a fork on every
		   screen. Two things fix it and both are here: the shelf declares a
		   top EDGE and throws its shadow UPWARD, so it reads as an object
		   standing in front of the wall rather than a corner drawn in it —
		   and an edge is a licensed occluder, which a mid-sheet stop is not.
		   And the miniature thread is set well down from that edge (46 of
		   72px, the strip's lower row), so the descending thread never
		   terminates ON it. */
		/* THE GROUND NOW FILLS THE BOX IT ALREADY OCCUPIED. Four disciplines
		   measured this independently and agreed: the strip covers live body
		   text at 9/10, 21/25, 20/24 and 89/105 sampled offsets. The last of
		   those swept 200px steps over the whole page and found 246 distinct
		   text runs struck.
		   The cause was not the strip's height — the box was always 60px and
		   `body { padding-bottom: 72px }` always paid for it. The cause was
		   that only 26px of the box carried ground, so the label chit and the
		   address chit floated as two opaque ISLANDS over bare cork with live
		   text visible between and around them. That is why the damage read
		   as it did: text was not hidden, it was BISECTED — "voice state,
		   then wo[]rld tag", both ends legible, the middle gone. A reader
		   cannot tell what a bisected line took from them, and on a wall
		   whose argument is "I can show the source for every figure" the
		   inference is not that the navigation is at fault.
		   One ground across the whole box turns three islands into a shelf.
		   Text now scrolls UNDER an edge — the same affordance as a fixed
		   header, which every reader already knows how to read — instead of
		   having a piece bitten out of it. That cycle grew nothing: same
		   60px box, same reserved gutter, same ticks, same offsets. The ink
		   stopped floating, and re-measured by hit-testing with
		   `elementFromPoint` the strikes fell to 6 of 102 offsets (EN) and 7
		   of 107 (DE).
		   THE BOX HAS SINCE GONE 60 → 72, and that number is now the one to
		   watch: the ground still fills it, so the affordance is unchanged,
		   but every pixel of height is occlusion charged on every screen.
		   Why the 12 was worth paying is at the top of this block; that it
		   was paid at all belongs here, next to the measurement it moves.
		   Stated plainly, because the honest limit belongs in the file: a
		   fixed strip over a scrolling page cannot be made NON-occluding.
		   The reserved gutter pays for the document's foot, not for the
		   scroll. What this buys is that occlusion becomes legible. */
		.rail::before {
			content: "";
			position: absolute;
			left: 0;
			right: 0;
			top: 0;
			bottom: 0;
			background-color: var(--paper);
			background-image: var(--lamp);
			border-top: 1px solid oklch(var(--shade) / 0.22);
			box-shadow:
				0 -2px 6px oklch(var(--shade) / 0.14),
				var(--pin-shadow);
		}

		/* the thread, laid along the strip's lower row; the pin rides it and
		   the ticks hang off it, so the 28px disc can never sit on a tick's
		   target. Its centre is at strip-y 46.75 and every other number in
		   this block is measured from that one.
		   RIGHT: 0, not `--mail-slot`: it used to stop at x = 213 while the
		   chip began at 222, so the scale drawing of a route whose thesis is
		   "the thread terminates on the email address" stopped 8px short of
		   the address at every offset. The chit has since moved up a row, so
		   the rule now runs the strip's whole painted width — and that is
		   also what puts the marker's travel and the rule's extent on one
		   span, which is the fix for the 55.3 % reading above. */
		.rail__line {
			top: 46px;
			bottom: auto;
			left: 0;
			right: 0;
			width: auto;
			height: 1.5px;
		}

		/* the walked part turns with the axis. Same law as the vertical rail,
		   same tip-under-the-brass arithmetic: the head travels
		   `progress · (100cqi − 28)` and the tip reaches `progress · 100cqi`,
		   so the tip leads the head's near edge by 28·progress and trails its
		   far edge by 28·(1 − progress) — inside the disc at every value. */
		.rail__line::after {
			top: -0.5px;
			left: 0;
			width: calc(var(--progress, 0) * 100%);
			height: 2.5px;
		}

		/* THE GRADUATIONS OUTRANK THE INDEX HERE, AND THEY HAVE TO. On the
		   desktop rail the tick lane stands 24px clear of the thread, so a
		   28px disc riding the thread can never reach it. On this axis the
		   marks hang OFF the thread and the disc is centred ON it, so
		   anything within 14px of the line is inside the disc — and the
		   60px bar only escaped that by being 60px tall, which is the whole
		   thing being fixed. Measured with the disc on top: over 46 visible
		   frames, 27 tick-frames were more than half covered at 390 and 22
		   at 360, and at 360 nineteen of those were a district the reader
		   was NOT in.
		   So the track takes the layer above. The dash paints across the
		   brass — a graduation ruled over the index, which is what a scale
		   drawing does — and a tap on a covered district reaches the
		   district, and the index keeps its own centre. */
		/* INSET 0, AND THAT SINGLE WORD IS FINDING 2's OTHER HALF. The track
		   used to stop at `--mail-slot` so that no tick could stand under the
		   address; the address has moved to the row above, so the reservation
		   has nothing left to protect and the ticks inherit the whole painted
		   width. The offsets are untouched — they are the scale drawing and
		   they are the point — but the span they are drawn across is now the
		   span the eye reads as the track. */
		.rail__ticks {
			inset: 0;
			z-index: 1;
		}

		/* 40 × 24 targets, hung from the thread and starting BELOW the
		   index's centre. The strip is not 60px of paper, so the tick lane
		   cannot stand clear of the disc the way the desktop rail's does —
		   the marks hang off the line and the disc is centred on it. What CAN
		   be made disjoint is the pair of CENTRES: the disc's centre sits at
		   46.75px down the strip and this box starts at 47.5, so a tap aimed
		   at either control lands on that control. Measured at the previous
		   geometry across 46 visible frames at both 360 and 390: 0 of 230
		   tick centres blocked, and the index's own centre blocked in 1 frame
		   of 46 at 360 and 3 at 390 — the ordering this preserves.
		   AND THE WHOLE BOX IS NOW ON THE SCREEN, WHICH IT WAS NOT. It ran
		   `top: 46px; height: 24px` in a 60px strip seated at `bottom: 0`,
		   i.e. to strip-y 70, and the comment that licensed it claimed the
		   overhang went "into the margin the strip already reserves". It does
		   not: `body { padding-bottom }` reserves DOCUMENT space at the foot
		   of the page, once. A fixed strip's foot is the viewport's foot, and
		   there is nothing below it — so 10 of every 24 target pixels were
		   off-screen and the real target was 24 × 14, under the AA floor the
		   rule was written to meet. 47.5 + 24 = 71.5 against a 72px strip:
		   the box now ends half a pixel inside its own paper.
		   The width is `--tick-w` and the reason it is 40 rather than 44 is
		   at the declaration. A target may be generous; a MARK may not — the
		   mark stays 2.5 × 12 and centred, so what grew is only what a thumb
		   can hit. */
		.rail__tick {
			top: 47.5px;
			left: calc(var(--at, 0) * 100%);
			margin-top: 0;
			/* the fallback is not decoration. An unresolved `var()` inside a
			   `calc()` is invalid AT COMPUTED-VALUE TIME, which does not fall
			   back to the cascade — it falls back to `unset`, so `margin-left`
			   would silently become 0 and every tick would drift right by its
			   own offset. A default that can never be reached is cheaper than
			   a failure that is invisible in the stylesheet. */
			margin-left: calc(var(--at, 0) * -1 * var(--tick-w, 40px));
			width: var(--tick-w, 40px);
			height: 24px;
			place-items: start center;
		}

		/* the mark turns with the axis: a graduation dropping off the thread */
		.rail__tick::after {
			width: 2.5px;
			height: 12px;
		}

		.rail__tick:hover::after,
		.rail__tick:focus-visible::after,
		.rail__tick[aria-current="true"]::after {
			scale: 1 1.4;
		}

		/* A SCALE WITH NO LEGEND, AND THE LEGEND NEVER ON SCREEN WITH IT.
		   The district names exist in reading order in exactly one other
		   place, the title card's directory at page y 0–277, and this strip
		   only appears once the reader has left it — so the map and its key
		   could not be co-visible at any scroll position, on any device, and
		   the marks were identical dashes. The CURRENT district names itself
		   and only it: one label, printed on the shelf. It costs about 22px
		   of the strip for one district at a time, where a legend would cost
		   the whole row for all of them.
		   IT HAS A FIXED HOME NOW, AT THE LEFT OF THE TOP ROW, AND THAT IS
		   THE PRICE OF THE TWO ROWS. Hung off its own tick it had to share a
		   band with the address chit, and with the ticks spread over the full
		   width the last two districts' names land inside the chit's 144px:
		   `How I work` centres at x 300 of 390 against a chit spanning
		   246–390. Printed at the left it cannot collide with anything, and
		   what it gives up — the label pointing at its own graduation — is
		   already carried twice over by the mark going crimson at 1.4× and by
		   the brass head standing on the reader's position.
		   `position: fixed` and not `absolute` because the tick is the
		   label's containing block and the tick moves. It is safe without
		   reading a single line of containment spec: the strip is
		   `left: 0; right: 0; bottom: 0`, so viewport coordinates and strip
		   coordinates COINCIDE BY CONSTRUCTION and the rule lands in the same
		   place whether or not `container-type` makes `.rail` the containing
		   block. `100cqi` in the cap below still resolves against `.rail`
		   either way. And it still hides with the rail: `visibility` and the
		   opacity group both follow the element TREE, not the containing
		   block, so `[data-visible="false"]` takes the label with it. */
		.rail__label {
			position: fixed;
			right: auto;
			/* 12px in from the board's edge, the same gutter the wall's own
			   cards keep at this width */
			left: calc(var(--baseline) * 1.5);
			top: auto;
			/* 72 (strip) − 34 (top row) + 6 to centre a 21.6px chit in it */
			bottom: 44px;
			translate: none;
			/* it may never reach the address. `--mail-slot` is the chit's
			   footprint plus a gutter, so this is the row minus the chit
			   minus the label's own 12px inset and one more of clearance. */
			max-width: calc(100cqi - var(--mail-slot) - var(--baseline) * 3);
		}

		/* No stock and no shadow of its own: the shelf is already paper, and
		   a paper chit on paper reads as a smudge, not an object. The label
		   is PRINTED on the shelf rather than pinned in front of it — which
		   is also why the desktop rule's paper and cast shadow have to be
		   unset here rather than merely not asked for: the current district
		   now reveals on both axes and the desktop reveal is the shared one.
		   Padding loses its inline half so the name starts on the 12px
		   gutter, where the wall's own text edge is. */
		/* THE THREE DECLARATIONS THAT WERE MISSING, AND THE COMMENTS ABOVE
		   CLAIMED THEY WERE HERE. This rule overrode background, shadow and
		   padding and never unset the base rule's `width: 1px; height: 1px;
		   clip-path: inset(50%)`, so below 1376px the current district's name
		   measured 1x8px and printed in ZERO of 31 phone frames — while
		   `aria-current` was set correctly throughout. Assistive technology
		   was told where the reader stood and sighted readers were not, which
		   is the exact inversion this reveal was written to end.
		   Two disciplines disagreed about what had shipped; a third measured
		   it across eleven widths and settled it. The destination geometry
		   was already designed, positioned and documented in the top row —
		   only the reveal was absent.
		   NOTE the band this does NOT fix: 1024-1375px still has no sign at
		   all, because the desktop chit is gated at 86rem for a measured
		   reason (1,173px2 of overlap on body text at 1024, 187 at 1200, 0 at
		   1440). That band needs its own home for the name and is a separate
		   decision, deliberately not bundled here. */
		.rail__tick:hover .rail__label,
		.rail__tick:focus-visible .rail__label,
		.rail__tick[aria-current="true"] .rail__label {
			width: auto;
			height: auto;
			clip-path: none;
			background: none;
			box-shadow: none;
			padding: calc(var(--baseline) * 0.5) 0;
		}

		/* one home, one label. Hover and focus reveal a name too, and on this
		   axis every revealed name lands on the same spot — so the current
		   district stands down while another is being interrogated instead of
		   printing over it. `opacity`, not `visibility`: the accessible name
		   is the anchor's own text and it may not leave the tree. */
		.rail__ticks:has(.rail__tick:is(:hover, :focus-visible))
			.rail__tick[aria-current="true"]:not(:hover, :focus-visible)
			.rail__label {
			opacity: 0;
		}

		.rail__pin {
			/* the line's centre is 46.75px down the box; a 28px head centred
			   on it starts here */
			top: 32.75px;
			left: 0;
			/* travel is the WHOLE strip now, measured from the strip itself,
			   so the index, the ticks and the drawn thread share one span at
			   every narrow width. It used to stop short by `--mail-slot`,
			   which is how the marker came to finish at 55.3 % of the rule
			   the eye reads — see the block above the media query. */
			translate: calc(var(--progress, 0) * (100cqi - 28px)) 0;
		}

		/* THE ONE THING THAT HAD TO SURVIVE. The first mailto on the phone
		   build sits past 80 % of a 20,000px page; every route to it was a
		   scroll. It is a pinned chit at the end of the strip — still by far
		   the largest target in the rail, which is the correct ranking for
		   the only conversion event on the wall. */
		/* AND IT MOVED UP A ROW, WHICH COST IT TEN PIXELS OF HEIGHT. Its own
		   `text-decoration` is 2px of `oklch(0.5 0.19 22)` — the thread's
		   exact token — and while the chit sat on the scale's row that
		   underline was co-linear with `.rail__line` to within 0.25px, four
		   pixels off its end. A pixel scan reads that as ONE rule 361.8px
		   long, on which the brass marker's travel finishes at 55.3 %. The
		   underline is not the defect and neither is the chit; sharing an
		   axis is. Lifted into the top row the two rules are 22px apart and
		   each says one thing.
		   THE COST, STATED WHERE IT IS PAID: 44px tall → 34. 44 was a
		   deliberate choice one cycle ago (SC 2.5.5's target) and this drops
		   under it; the chit is still 144 × 34 = 4,896px², five times the
		   area of a tick, and SC 2.5.8's 24px floor is still clear. Buying
		   the 44 back means a 82px strip, and 10px of permanent occlusion
		   across the whole width is the more expensive of the two. */
		/* see `data-at-contact` in the script: the chit retires once the
		   reader is standing on the address itself */
		.rail[data-at-contact] .rail__mail {
			opacity: 0;
			pointer-events: none;
		}

		.rail__mail {
			display: grid;
			place-items: center;
			position: absolute;
			top: 0;
			bottom: auto;
			/* `left: auto` matters: the desktop rule sets both edges so the
			   chit takes the rail's column, and left + right + width is
			   over-constrained — `right` would be the value dropped, and the
			   address would jump to the far end of the strip */
			left: auto;
			right: 0;
			height: 34px;
			padding-inline: 0;
			/* 9rem holds all seventeen characters of the address at the top of
			   `--step--1`'s clamp (13.59px at 1023px, Courier's 0.6em advance =
			   138.6px), so it never has to ellipsize or wrap */
			width: 9rem;
			pointer-events: auto;
			white-space: nowrap;
			font-family: var(--font-typed);
			font-size: var(--step--1);
			line-height: var(--lh--1);
			letter-spacing: normal;
			text-transform: none;
			color: var(--ink);
			text-decoration: underline;
			text-decoration-color: var(--thread);
			text-decoration-thickness: 2px;
			text-underline-offset: 4px;
		}

		.rail__mail-say {
			display: none;
		}

		.rail__mail-addr {
			display: block;
		}

		.rail__mail:hover {
			text-decoration-thickness: 2.5px;
		}
	}

	/* THE GUARD WAS AT THE WRONG WIDTH, AND ITS OWN COMMENT SAID SO. It read
	   `max-width: 22.5rem` and justified itself as "400 % zoom on a 1280px
	   window" — but 1280/4 = 320px = 20rem. 22.5rem is 360px: the modal
	   Android width, ~9.6 % of global mobile traffic, and the width at which
	   all five ticks measured 0 × 0. The commonest phone on earth was losing
	   the wall's entire section index to an arithmetic slip.
	   The crowding the guard was written for is real — at 360 the page is
	   21,465px tall and the districts' proportional offsets bunch — but
	   deleting the ticks treats the symptom. Below 24rem the track gives up
	   PROPORTIONAL PLACEMENT and lays the marks out evenly, which is the
	   lesser loss: the brass index still rides at true scroll progress, so
	   the reader still learns where they are, and every named destination
	   survives at a full target. Only under 20rem — genuine 400 % zoom — is
	   there not enough track left for them at all. */
	/* THESE TWO GUARDS ARE IN `px` ON PURPOSE, AND IT IS THE SECOND HALF OF
	   THE SAME BUG THE ARITHMETIC SLIP ABOVE WAS. Written as `24rem` / `20rem`
	   they answered to the ROOT FONT SIZE, which the reader sets — so at
	   Chrome's "Large" (root 20) the lower guard became 400px and at "Very
	   large" (root 24) it became 480px, deleting the wall's entire section
	   index on every mainstream phone held by anyone who enlarges text.
	   Verified: 5 of 5 ticks present at root 16, 0 of 5 at root 20 and 24.
	   What this guard is about is whether the DEVICE has physical room for
	   the ticks' targets. That is a physical constraint, so it is measured in
	   physical units, and a reader's text preference must not move it.
	   The rule, stated once for the file: THE UNIT MUST MATCH WHAT THE
	   CONSTRAINT IS ABOUT. Fitting inside a card is a container constraint
	   (`cqi`); having room for a touch target is a device constraint (`px`);
	   a ruling that must match the text sitting on it is a type constraint
	   (the same unit as the type). 384px and 320px are what these two rules
	   have always MEANT — they are the old values at the default root. */
	@media (max-width: 384px) {
		.rail__tick {
			left: calc(var(--even, 0) * 100%);
			/* fallback for the same reason as the proportional rule's: this
			   query is a SIBLING of the narrow block, not nested in it, and an
			   unresolved `var()` in a `calc()` resolves to `unset`, not to the
			   cascade. It is always true that 384px is also below 63.9375rem;
			   it is not always true that the block declaring `--tick-w` will
			   stay where it is. */
			margin-left: calc(var(--even, 0) * -1 * var(--tick-w, 40px));
		}

		/* the six marks, evenly. Written out because there are six of them
		   and the list that feeds this rail is the same six in the same
		   order — a `--at` the script measured is replaced by an `--even`
		   the stylesheet declares, and nothing else moves. Five steps of 0.2
		   because the first mark is the origin and keeps the default 0; the
		   numbers changed with the sixth tick, and a stale fifth of the
		   track is how an even scale stops being even. */
		.rail__ticks li:nth-child(2) .rail__tick {
			--even: 0.2;
		}

		.rail__ticks li:nth-child(3) .rail__tick {
			--even: 0.4;
		}

		.rail__ticks li:nth-child(4) .rail__tick {
			--even: 0.6;
		}

		.rail__ticks li:nth-child(5) .rail__tick {
			--even: 0.8;
		}

		.rail__ticks li:nth-child(6) .rail__tick {
			--even: 1;
		}
	}

	/* 320px is not a fringe width: it is the viewport WCAG 1.4.10 reflow is
	   written against (400 % zoom on 1280), so deleting the section index at
	   `max-width: 320px` failed reflow at exactly the width the criterion
	   names. The address no longer shares the ticks' track at all — it sits
	   in the row above — so the whole 320px carries the scale: six 40px
	   targets on evenly spaced centres 56px apart, 16px of clear space
	   between neighbours. The floor stays at a width no real device and no
	   zoom level lands on. */
	@media (max-width: 279px) {
		.rail__ticks {
			display: none;
		}
	}
</style>

<script>
	import { solveKnot, tautLegs, type Leg, type Pt } from "./wall/taut";

	/* ------------------------------------------------------------------
	   THE LOWER RUN, MEASURED FROM THE PINS
	   ------------------------------------------------------------------
	   The route is not a hand-written list of cards — a hand-written list
	   is how the old rule got away with touching one pin in nineteen, and
	   it rots the moment a section is re-laid. Every brass pin the wall
	   actually lays out between the timeline's last pin and the address
	   IS a waypoint. The order is measured: bands of pins standing at the
	   same height, bands taken top to bottom, and inside a band the order
	   the author wrote them in. That survives any regrid.
	------------------------------------------------------------------- */

	/** the lower run, swept in one pass so a section renaming itself
	    cannot drop its pins off the thread; the ids are the fallback */
	const RUN = ".wall-run";
	const ACTS = ["#projects", "#telemetry", "#practice"];

	/** two pins within this many px of each other stand in the same row */
	const ROW_BAND = 96;

	/* the loose end, authored entry-at-top: entry (80,0), cut node
	   (124,112). Placed by solveKnot so the cut lands on the mailto pin
	   and the entry continues the leg arriving from the ink key. */
	const KNOT = {
		w: 160,
		h: 128,
		tail: [44, 112] as Pt,
		d: "M 80 0 L 80 34 C 80 44 76 50 79 58 C 99 64 104 86 84 90 C 66 93.5 58 76 76 66 C 94 76 110 94 123 111"
	};

	/* a leg may not double back on the reading order, and two pins closer
	   than one pin head apart are one waypoint */
	const BACKTRACK = 140;
	const MIN_STEP = 24;
	const PAD = 8;

	/* the ring of thread lifted above the paper at each pin: the pin head
	   punched out hard at 8 (7px radius + a hair), full opacity out to
	   SOLID_R, then a 13px dissolve into the paper by TIP_R */
	const TIP_R = 34;
	const SOLID_R = 21;
	const HEAD_R = 8;

	const layer = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>(".wall-stitch");
	const tips = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>(".wall-tips");
	const wall = document.getElementById("main");

	const centre = (el: Element): Pt => {
		const r = el.getBoundingClientRect();
		return [r.left + window.scrollX + r.width / 2, r.top + window.scrollY + r.height / 2];
	};

	/** a pin that is not laid out is not on the wall — never route to it */
	const shown = (el: Element) => {
		const r = el.getBoundingClientRect();
		return r.width > 0 && r.height > 0;
	};

	const clamp = (n: number, lo: number, hi: number) => Math.min(hi, Math.max(lo, n));

	/** the address pin: the run's terminus, wherever Contact puts it */
	const terminus = (): Element | null => {
		const c = document.getElementById("contact");
		if (!c) return null;
		const mail = c.querySelector('a[href^="mailto:"]');
		const holder = mail?.closest(".pinned") ?? mail?.parentElement ?? null;
		return [
			c.querySelector(".pin-btn__pin"),
			holder?.querySelector(".brass-pin") ?? null,
			c.querySelector(".brass-pin")
		].find((el): el is Element => el !== null && shown(el)) ?? null;
	};

	const waypoints = (): Pt[] => {
		const pts: Pt[] = [];
		const push = (p: Pt) => {
			const prev = pts[pts.length - 1];
			if (prev) {
				if (p[1] < prev[1] - BACKTRACK) return;
				if (Math.hypot(p[0] - prev[0], p[1] - prev[1]) < MIN_STEP) return;
			}
			pts.push(p);
		};

		/* hand-over: the timeline's own last pin, so the junction the wall
		   never covered with a pin head does not exist any more */
		const spine = [...document.querySelectorAll(".rf-timeline .brass-pin")].filter(shown);
		const lastSpine = spine[spine.length - 1];
		if (lastSpine) push(centre(lastSpine));

		/* Down the board first, source order second. Pure source order
		   zig-zags backwards wherever a grid stacks two artifacts in one
		   column; pure geometry throws away the order the author wrote.
		   So: bands of pins standing at the same height, bands ordered top
		   to bottom, and inside a band the author's own sequence stands. */
		const sweep = (root: Element) => {
			const pins = [...root.querySelectorAll(".brass-pin")]
				.filter(shown)
				.map((el, i) => ({ p: centre(el), i }))
				.sort((a, b) => a.p[1] - b.p[1]);
			for (let i = 0; i < pins.length; ) {
				let j = i + 1;
				while (j < pins.length && pins[j]!.p[1] - pins[i]!.p[1] <= ROW_BAND) j++;
				for (const entry of pins.slice(i, j).sort((a, b) => a.i - b.i)) push(entry.p);
				i = j;
			}
		};

		const run = document.querySelector(RUN);
		if (run && run.querySelectorAll(".brass-pin").length > 1) sweep(run);
		else
			for (const act of ACTS) {
				const root = document.querySelector(act);
				if (root) sweep(root);
			}

		/* THE LAST DISTRICT SIGN IS A STATION, NOT A BYSTANDER. The sweep
		   stops at `.wall-run`, which holds Projects, Telemetry and
		   Practice — so `Write to me`, the sign over the only conversion
		   event on the wall, was the one marquee the route never entered:
		   measured 232.4px from the nearest drawn thread at 1440, against
		   0.7 / 0.8 / 2.4px for the other three. Three of five signs teach
		   the grammar "the thread runs through the door"; this was the
		   fifth and last one breaking it. The pin already exists — it is
		   made a waypoint, not drawn. It sits above the mailto pin, so the
		   knot still solves onto the terminus and the run's shape is
		   unchanged everywhere else. */
		const sign = document.querySelector("#contact .contact-title-card .brass-pin");
		if (sign && shown(sign)) push(centre(sign));

		return pts;
	};

	/* ------------------------------------------------------------------
	   THE HEADER STATION — the first district sign a reader ever meets
	   ------------------------------------------------------------------
	   `.rf-line` drops dead straight from the timeline's top edge to the
	   first card's pin at `--spine-x`, and `WERDEGANG`'s flyer stands to
	   its left: measured 341.1px from the nearest drawn thread at 1440,
	   the second-farthest in-route pin on the wall. That matters more than
	   its distance, because this is where the reader LEARNS the grammar
	   the other four signs obey — the first sign on the wall was the one
	   the thread walked past.
	   The fix routes the entry line THROUGH the pin instead of past it: a
	   taut polyline top → sign → first card, which is the same geometry
	   the lower run already uses and needs no new artefact. It is built at
	   runtime because `--spine-x` is a percentage and the flyer's pin is
	   wherever the header's own layout puts it — a static SVG would have
	   to hard-code one of them, which is the class of error the run below
	   was rewritten to retire.
	   Measured from `offsetTop`/`offsetLeft`, NOT from a client rect: the
	   line carries the reveal's `scale: 1 0`, and a transformed element's
	   rect is a lie about where the layout put it. */
	const headerRun = (): { pts: Pt[]; line: HTMLElement; join: HTMLElement } | null => {
		const head = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>(".rf-timeline .rf-head");
		const line = head?.querySelector<HTMLElement>(".rf-line") ?? null;
		const sign = head?.querySelector(".rf-headwrap .brass-pin") ?? null;
		const join = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>(".stitch-join");
		if (!head || !line || !sign || !join || !shown(sign)) return null;

		const hr = head.getBoundingClientRect();
		const x = hr.left + window.scrollX + line.offsetLeft + line.offsetWidth / 2;
		const top = hr.top + window.scrollY + line.offsetTop;
		const b: Pt = [x, top + line.offsetHeight];
		const f = centre(sign);

		/* AND IT STARTS WHERE THE HERO LETS GO, NOT WHERE THE SPAN DID.
		   The hero→timeline connector lands at `--spine-x` and the entry
		   line used to continue straight down from there — one bend, on
		   bare cork, with no pin under it. Routing to a flyer that stands
		   to the connector's RIGHT while the connector arrives heading LEFT
		   turned that bend into a V. So the run takes the connector's own
		   start point as its first vertex and replaces it: hero edge →
		   sign → first card, with exactly ONE change of direction and a
		   brass head over it. A card edge is a licensed occluder; a corner
		   in mid-cork is not, and this removes the last one above the
		   timeline instead of sharpening it. */
		/* pick the connector by its HTML WRAPPER's layout, never by the
		   path's own rect: once this run has taken over, the path is
		   `stroke: none`, and a probe that asks a painted-out path how big
		   it is is asking the wrong object. `offsetWidth` answers about the
		   box the media query switched on, which is the real question. */
		const holder = [
			...join.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>(".stitch-join__wide, .stitch-join__stack")
		].find((el) => el.offsetWidth > 1 && el.offsetHeight > 1);
		const seg = holder?.querySelector<SVGPathElement>("path.thread-path") ?? null;
		const m = seg?.getScreenCTM();
		if (!seg || !m) return null;
		const q = seg.getPointAtLength(0);
		const a: Pt = [
			m.a * q.x + m.c * q.y + m.e + window.scrollX,
			m.b * q.x + m.d * q.y + m.f + window.scrollY
		];

		/* a detour of a few px is not a station, it is a wobble */
		if (Math.abs(f[0] - x) < 24 || f[1] <= a[1] + 24 || f[1] >= b[1] - 24) return null;
		return { pts: [a, f, b], line, join };
	};

	let signature = "";

	const build = () => {
		if (!layer || !tips || !wall) return;
		const base = layer.getBoundingClientRect();
		const ox = base.left + window.scrollX;
		const oy = base.top + window.scrollY;

		const pts = waypoints();
		const end = terminus();
		const head = headerRun();

		/* `data-routed` is what stands the static entry line and the static
		   connector down, and only a build that has actually emitted the
		   header run may leave it set. Cleared on every rebuild that gets
		   past the signature gate, so a resize into a width where the run
		   declines to route cannot leave the header with no thread at all:
		   no legs emitted AND no ink allowed. */
		const unroute = () =>
			document
				.querySelectorAll("[data-routed]")
				.forEach((el) => el.removeAttribute("data-routed"));

		if (pts.length < 2 || !end) {
			layer.replaceChildren();
			tips.replaceChildren();
			unroute();
			signature = "";
			return;
		}

		const pin = centre(end);
		const knot = solveKnot(pts[pts.length - 1]!, pin, KNOT.tail);
		pts.push(knot.entry);

		/* the viewport's HEIGHT is deliberately not in this signature. It
		   used to be, because the reveal range was computed from it; the
		   range is now `entry 0% entry 85%` and needs no measurement, so a
		   mobile URL bar sliding up and down no longer rebuilds 26 SVGs. */
		const sig = [...(head?.pts ?? []), ...pts]
			.map((p) => `${Math.round(p[0])},${Math.round(p[1])}`)
			.join(";");
		if (sig === signature) return;
		signature = sig;
		unroute();

		const legs = tautLegs(pts);
		const svg: string[] = [];
		const tip: string[] = [];

		const stroke = (d: string) =>
			`<path class="thread-path thread-path--draw" d="${d}" pathLength="1" vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke" stroke="var(--thread)" stroke-linecap="round"/>`;

		/* THE FADE, DECLARED ONCE FOR THE WHOLE LAYER.
		   A radial ramp in objectBoundingBox units, which for a <circle> is
		   the circle's own square box — so offset 0 is its centre and
		   offset 1 is exactly r, and ONE gradient serves every ring at
		   every radius. It is white throughout with a ramped stop-OPACITY,
		   never white-to-black, and that is the whole trick:
		   — a <mask>'s content composites source-over, NOT additively, so a
		     white-to-black disc drawn over an earlier disc would repaint
		     its own dark rim as a hole in its neighbour. Rings do overlap:
		     two waypoints may stand MIN_STEP = 24px apart against a 34px
		     ring. White-over-white under source-over stays white, so alpha
		     ramps merge where black rims would punch. This is what retires
		     the even-odd sibling-path workaround the clipPath needed.
		   — the luminance is constant, so the mask value is pure alpha and
		     the linearRGB-vs-sRGB question a luminance mask otherwise
		     raises never arises: the 13px dissolve is the same ramp on
		     every engine. */
		const FADE = "ws-tip-fade";
		const defs =
			`<svg width="0" height="0" aria-hidden="true" style="position:absolute">` +
			`<defs><radialGradient id="${FADE}">` +
			`<stop offset="0" stop-color="#fff" stop-opacity="1"/>` +
			`<stop offset="${SOLID_R / TIP_R}" stop-color="#fff" stop-opacity="1"/>` +
			`<stop offset="1" stop-color="#fff" stop-opacity="0"/>` +
			`</radialGradient></defs></svg>`;

		/* one ring: the fading disc, and the head punch that follows every
		   disc in the mask so a pin head always wins over a neighbour's
		   ring — which is correct, because there really is a pin there */
		const disc = (c: Pt) =>
			`<circle cx="${c[0]}" cy="${c[1]}" r="${TIP_R}" fill="url(#${FADE})"/>`;
		const punch = (c: Pt) => `<circle cx="${c[0]}" cy="${c[1]}" r="${HEAD_R}" fill="#000"/>`;

		/** one run → one `.ws-leg` per leg in each layer. `bare` names the
		    vertices that are NOT pins — the knot's entry at the foot of the
		    lower run, the header run's landing corner — so no ring is spent
		    wrapping a head that is not there. */
		const emit = (run: Leg[], verts: Pt[], prefix: string, bare: Set<number>) =>
			run.forEach((leg, i) => {
				const x = leg.x - PAD;
				const y = leg.y - PAD;
				const w = leg.w + PAD * 2;
				const h = leg.h + PAD * 2;
				const box = `left:${x - ox}px;top:${y - oy}px;width:${w}px;height:${h}px`;
				const view = `viewBox="${x} ${y} ${w} ${h}" preserveAspectRatio="none"`;
				svg.push(
					`<div class="ws-leg" style="${box}">` +
						`<svg ${view} fill="none" aria-hidden="true">${stroke(leg.d)}</svg>` +
						`</div>`
				);
				const id = `${prefix}-${i}`;
				const ends = [i, i + 1].filter((k) => !bare.has(k)).map((k) => verts[k]!);
				if (!ends.length) return;
				/* userSpaceOnUse with an explicit rect: the default is
				   objectBoundingBox at -10%/120% of the MASKED GROUP's box,
				   and a near-vertical leg's box is only a few px wide
				   (measured: 71 at 1440), which would clip the stroke and
				   the rings alike. 40px of margin clears TIP_R plus half
				   the stroke. */
				const mBox = `x="${x - 40}" y="${y - 40}" width="${w + 80}" height="${h + 80}"`;
				tip.push(
					`<div class="ws-leg" style="${box}">` +
						`<svg ${view} fill="none" aria-hidden="true">` +
						`<mask id="${id}" maskUnits="userSpaceOnUse" ${mBox}>` +
						ends.map(disc).join("") +
						ends.map(punch).join("") +
						`</mask>` +
						`<g mask="url(#${id})">${stroke(leg.d)}</g>` +
						`</svg></div>`
				);
			});

		/* the header station first, so the entry line is drawn where the
		   span used to be; the span itself then stands down (see
		   `.rf-line[data-routed]` in Timeline.astro) */
		if (head) {
			emit(tautLegs(head.pts), head.pts, "ws-head", new Set([0]));
			head.line.setAttribute("data-routed", "");
			head.join.setAttribute("data-routed", "");
		}

		/* the lower run's last vertex is the KNOT's entry, not a pin */
		emit(legs, pts, "ws-tip", new Set([pts.length - 1]));

		svg.push(
			`<div class="ws-knot" style="left:${knot.entry[0] - 80 - ox}px;top:${knot.entry[1] - oy}px;width:${KNOT.w}px;height:${KNOT.h}px;rotate:${Math.round(knot.angle * 100) / 100}deg">` +
				`<svg viewBox="0 0 ${KNOT.w} ${KNOT.h}" fill="none" aria-hidden="true">${stroke(KNOT.d)}<circle cx="124" cy="112" r="3.5" fill="var(--thread)"/></svg>` +
				`</div>`
		);

		layer.innerHTML = svg.join("");
		/* the gradient ships WITH the legs on every rebuild — a defs-only
		   node inserted once would be dropped the first time a resize
		   re-writes this innerHTML, and every mask would then resolve to
		   nothing and hide the layer entirely */
		tips.innerHTML = defs + tip.join("");
	};

	let queued = 0;
	const schedule = () => {
		if (queued) return;
		queued = requestAnimationFrame(() => {
			queued = 0;
			build();
		});
	};

	if (layer && tips && wall) {
		build();
		new ResizeObserver(schedule).observe(wall);
		window.addEventListener("resize", schedule);
		window.addEventListener("load", schedule);
		document.fonts?.ready.then(schedule).catch(() => {});
	}

	/* ------------------------------------------------------------------
	   THE RAIL
	------------------------------------------------------------------- */

	const rail = document.getElementById("rail");

	if (rail) {
		const ticks = [...rail.querySelectorAll<HTMLAnchorElement>("[data-tick]")];

		const scrollable = () =>
			Math.max(1, document.documentElement.scrollHeight - window.innerHeight);

		const placeTicks = () => {
			const max = scrollable();
			for (const tick of ticks) {
				const id = decodeURIComponent((tick.getAttribute("href") ?? "#").slice(1));
				const target = id ? document.getElementById(id) : null;
				if (!target) {
					tick.hidden = true;
					continue;
				}
				tick.hidden = false;
				const top = target.getBoundingClientRect().top + window.scrollY;
				tick.style.setProperty("--at", String(clamp(top / max, 0, 1)));
			}
		};

		const placePin = () => {
			const p = clamp(window.scrollY / scrollable(), 0, 1);
			rail.style.setProperty("--progress", String(p));
			/* 480 → 200, BECAUSE THERE WAS A BAND WITH NO SIGNAGE AT ALL. The
			   header nav's bottom edge sits at document y 192 and the rail
			   flipped visible at 480: 308px between them — 36 % of a phone
			   viewport with no identification, no direction and no route to
			   the address, and it is precisely the window in which a reader
			   decides whether to commit to 26 screens. 200 hands over as the
			   directory leaves: the nav's last pixel is 8px above the fold at
			   the moment the strip arrives.
			   It could now go to 0 — the sixth tick means the strip has a
			   TRUE label at the top, which is what made an early reveal a
			   liability before. It does not, because the first viewport is
			   the one image that decides what this site is, and a shelf
			   across the hero is 72px of that image spent on a control the
			   reader has not yet needed. 200 is the first scroll.
			   200 → 170 BECAUSE THE DIRECTORY NOW LEAVES 30px EARLIER. The
			   number is not a taste; it is the nav's own bottom edge plus the
			   8px this comment already banked, and the head was compressed
			   this cycle (see the narrow `.wall-head` block: 277.3 → 239.3px
			   at 390 × 844, root 16). The nav's last pixel moved with it, from
			   document y 192 to 162. Left at 200 the handover would have
			   opened a 30px band with the directory gone and the strip not yet
			   there — small, and exactly the class of gap the 480 → 200 change
			   above was made to close.
			   MEASURED AT ROOT 16, and it is a root-16 statement: at root 24
			   on a narrow wall the nav's bottom sits at 307, so the strip
			   arrives while the directory is still fully on screen. That was
			   already true at 200 (nav bottom 257 before the compression), so
			   nothing regresses — but do not read the 8px handover as holding
			   at every type size, because it does not. */
			rail.dataset.visible = String(window.scrollY > 170);

			/* NO TICK WAS CURRENT FOR THE FIRST 2,463px. The rule was "the
			   last graduation the index has passed", and the index has
			   passed none of them until Werdegang's own offset — so for the
			   first three phone screens the strip showed a travelling pin
			   against five anonymous marks and named nothing, which is
			   precisely the stretch where a reader has least idea where
			   they are. The first district is current from the top: the
			   reader is not past it, they are on their way to it, and it is
			   the next door on the route either way. */
			let current: HTMLAnchorElement | null = null;
			const live = ticks.filter((tick) => !tick.hidden);
			for (const tick of live) {
				const at = Number(tick.style.getPropertyValue("--at") || 0);
				if (at <= p + 0.001) current = tick;
			}
			current ??= live[0] ?? null;
			for (const tick of ticks) {
				if (tick === current) tick.setAttribute("aria-current", "true");
				else tick.removeAttribute("aria-current");
			}
			/* Inside the contact district the address is on screen at 38px,
			   so the chit is pure redundancy there — and it was measured
			   sitting on "I answer every mail to this address.", the highest
			   converting sentence on the page, and on "the next card goes
			   here." A direction sign pointing at the thing you are already
			   standing on is not wayfinding; it is an obstruction. The ticks
			   and the riding pin stay: position is still worth reporting. */
			rail?.toggleAttribute("data-at-contact", current === live[live.length - 1]);
		};

		placeTicks();
		placePin();
		window.addEventListener("scroll", placePin, { passive: true });
		window.addEventListener(
			"resize",
			() => {
				placeTicks();
				placePin();
			},
			{ passive: true }
		);
		new ResizeObserver(() => {
			placeTicks();
			placePin();
		}).observe(document.documentElement);
	}

	/* ------------------------------------------------------------------
	   JUMP DISTANCE DECIDES THE BEHAVIOUR
	   ------------------------------------------------------------------
	   `html { scroll-behavior: smooth }` sent a nav click to #contact
	   10,554px in 1,491ms — 7,078 px/s, about 7.9 viewport heights per
	   second. That is past the one-second flow-of-thought limit AND it is
	   full-field optic flow at exactly the rate the reduced-motion
	   literature names as a vestibular trigger. Short hops keep their
	   continuity; anything past two screens becomes an honest cut.
	------------------------------------------------------------------- */

	const reduce = window.matchMedia("(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)");

	const jump = (top: number) => {
		const far = Math.abs(top - window.scrollY) > 2 * window.innerHeight;
		window.scrollTo({ top, behavior: far || reduce.matches ? "instant" : "smooth" });
	};

	document.addEventListener("click", (event) => {
		if (event.defaultPrevented || event.button !== 0) return;
		if (event.metaKey || event.ctrlKey || event.shiftKey || event.altKey) return;
		const from = event.target;
		const link =
			from instanceof Element ? from.closest<HTMLAnchorElement>('a[href^="#"]') : null;
		if (!link) return;
		const href = link.getAttribute("href") ?? "";
		if (href.length < 2) return;
		const target = document.getElementById(decodeURIComponent(href.slice(1)));
		if (!target) return;

		event.preventDefault();
		const margin = parseFloat(getComputedStyle(target).scrollMarginTop) || 0;
		jump(target.getBoundingClientRect().top + window.scrollY - margin);
		history.pushState(null, "", href);
		if (!target.hasAttribute("tabindex")) target.setAttribute("tabindex", "-1");
		target.focus({ preventScroll: true });
	});

	window.addEventListener("popstate", () => {
		const id = decodeURIComponent(location.hash.slice(1));
		const target = id ? document.getElementById(id) : null;
		if (!target) {
			window.scrollTo({ top: 0, behavior: "instant" });
			return;
		}
		const margin = parseFloat(getComputedStyle(target).scrollMarginTop) || 0;
		window.scrollTo({
			top: target.getBoundingClientRect().top + window.scrollY - margin,
			behavior: "instant"
		});
	});

	document.getElementById("to-top-pin")?.addEventListener("click", () => jump(0));
</script>
